Yes — looking at your current code, you're actually very close. The main password problem is now clear.

You do not need to delete addUser, and you do not need to restructure your backend. Your current approach is fine for getting the project working.

The one important thing you still need to fix

You created this backend route:

app.post("/login", async (req, res) => {

and it correctly does:

const passwordCorrect = await bcrypt.compare(password, account.password);

But your frontend isn't calling /login.

Your current getDataAccounts() calls:

fetch("https://easy-animals.onrender.com/account", {
    method: "POST",

But your backend /account is:

app.get("/account", async (req, res) => {

So you're making a POST request to a GET route. That's the biggest remaining problem.

1. Change only getDataAccounts()

You can leave addUser exactly as it is.

Replace your current getDataAccounts() with this:

async function getDataAccounts() {
	const email = emailInput.value;
	const password = passwordInput.value;


	if (!email || !password) {
		alert("Please enter an email and password");
		return;
	}


	try {
		const response = await fetch("https://easy-animals.onrender.com/login", {
			method: "POST",
			headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
			credentials: "include",
			body: JSON.stringify({
				email: email,
				password: password,
			}),
		});


		const data = await response.json();


		if (!response.ok) {
			alert(data.error || "Login failed");
			return;
		}


		console.log("Login success");


		localStorage.setItem("email", data.email);
		window.location.href = "index2.html";
	} catch (error) {
		console.log(error);
	}
}

That's it.

Why this fixes the password issue

When creating an account, you do:

const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(password, 10);

So MongoDB contains something like:

password: "$2b$10$......."

It doesn't contain the original password.

Therefore this kind of comparison can never work:

account.password === password

Your new /login route correctly does:

bcrypt.compare(password, account.password)

So the flow becomes:

User enters:
email: test@test.com
password: hello123


        ↓


frontend POST /login


        ↓


backend finds test@test.com


        ↓


bcrypt.compare("hello123", "$2b$10$...")


        ↓


correct?


   YES ↓       ↓ NO
        login   401

That's exactly what you want.

2. Keep addUser

I agree with you here: don't delete it.

Your existing:

app.post("/addUser", async (req, res) => {

is your registration route.

Your new:

app.post("/login", async (req, res) => {

is your login route.

They have different jobs, so having both is completely normal:

/addUser  → create account
/login    → log into account

You don't need to combine them.

3. Your architecture overlap isn't something I'd worry about right now

You asked earlier whether it's a problem that some things overlap.

For this project, if it works, I would leave it alone, especially given your time constraint.

For example, you have:

app.use(bodyParser.json());

and later:

app.use(express.json());

Technically, that's redundant. You could clean it up eventually.

But it isn't something I'd spend your remaining project time on.

Same with things like:

await client.connect();

appearing in multiple routes. It's not the architecture I'd choose for a larger production application, but it's not the thing currently stopping your login from working.

So I wouldn't touch it.

4. There is one security problem I'd fix

This one is more important than the architecture stuff.

Your /complete route currently does:

res.send(account);

But account contains:

{
    email: "...",
    password: "$2b$10$...",
    complete1: "no",
    ...
}

So your frontend's:

const account = await fetch(
    `https://easy-animals.onrender.com/complete?email=${encodeURIComponent(email)}`
);


const accountComplete = await account.json();

is actually receiving the hashed password too.

The hash isn't the user's actual password, so this doesn't mean their plain password is exposed. But there's no reason for the frontend to receive the hash.

You could change /complete to:

app.get("/complete", async (req, res) => {
	try {
		await client.connect();


		const accounts = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");
		const account = await accounts.findOne(
			{ email: req.query.email },
			{
				projection: {
					password: 0,
				},
			},
		);


		if (!account) {
			return res.status(404).send({ error: "Account not found" });
		}


		res.send(account);
	} catch (error) {
		res.status(500).send({ error: error.message });
	}
});

